def rotate_clockwise(grid):
    return [list(column) for column in zip(*grid[::-1])]

`grid[::-1]` is a new list of the same row objects in reverse order, so the input is never mutated. Unpacking it into `zip` passes each row as a separate argument, and zip yields one tuple per column: the first tuple holds the old bottom-to-top first column, which is exactly the first row of the clockwise rotation. For an empty grid, `zip()` yields nothing and the result is []. Time and extra space are O(R x C), which is optimal because every cell is written once.

